  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nRadar trends early this morning depicts the bulk of leftover\nshowers prompted by the remnants of Isaias now located over the\nMid-Atlantic region. Excessive moisture trickling down from the\nnorthwest casts widespread stratus over the forecast area\nfeaturing embedded drizzle. Forecast soundings depict a very\nsaturated thermal profile early this morning across all climate\nsites that is progged to slowly erode throughout today as mid-\nlevel dry air presses down towards the surface. This signature\nholds through early this morning then breaks down from west to\neast heading into the afternoon, and eventually becoming\ncompressed along the higher terrain this evening.\n\nSunshine will then return late this afternoon across the Tri-\nState area, allowing daytime temperatures to return closer to\ntheir climatological norm for this time of year. Clearing skies\ntonight and lowering surface flow may allow for patchy river \nvalley fog along the higher terrain late tonight into Tuesday \nmorning.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nUpper level ridging sliding into the Gulf coast and surface high\npressure parked over the Carolinas will yield a period of dry\nweather on Tuesday into Tuesday night. Ample sunshine and\nonshore flow will promote afternoon highs to span the 70s and\n80s ahead of active weather returning once more for midweek.\n\nThe ridge loses influence over the Central Appalachians on\nWednesday as a deepening trough over the Four Corners drives a\ncold front down from the Great Lakes region. Showers and\nthunderstorms attached to the front encroach from the north by\nthe later half of Wednesday and looks to stall somewhere in\nclose vicinity to the forecast area heading into Thursday.\nInstability looks meager with this feature, which may help to \nsuppress concerns for severe weather.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nThe second half of the work week maintains unsettled weather as\nthe stationary boundary lifts as a warm front sometime on\nThursday, followed by a succession of cold fronts Friday into \nSaturday. This unlocks the door for a surge of cold air to pivot\ndown from Canada and yield the coldest temperatures forecast so\nfar this season. Post-frontal northwest flow will also usher in\nlake enhanced moisture that could stir up snow showers along \nthe ridges of our northeast mountain zones. Additionally, sub- \nto near-freezing temperatures overspreading the forecast area \nFriday night into Saturday may promote an areawide freeze that \ncould bring an end to the growing season.",
